<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seljuk Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Seljuks, Seljuk Turks, Great Seljuk Empire, Seljuk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Seljuk Empire, or Great Seljuk Empire, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Turkic state founded in the eleventh century by descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-warlord">Seljuk Beg</a>. It conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a> in 1071, opening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> to Turkish settlement. It later fragmented into regional states such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>, and was a forerunner of the Khwarazmian and Ottoman empires.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Military power increasingly devolved to provincial commanders and then to outright successor dynasties: the Tahirids, Saffarids and Samanids in the east, and later the Buyids and <b>Seljuks</b> at the centre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>More followed the Byzantine annexation of Greater Armenia in 1045 and its conquest by the <b>Seljuk Turks</b> nineteen years later: after Manzikert in 1071 Malik-Shah's governors laid repressive taxes on the Armenians, who fled west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The iqta system spread under the Buyids and later became the basis of military administration under the <b>Seljuks</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The decisive blow to Byzantine military power came at the Battle of Manzikert in 1071, where the <b>Seljuk Turks</b> under Alp Arslan defeated and captured Emperor Romanos IV Diogenes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke William IX led a large army on the Crusade of 1101, which was destroyed by the <b>Seljuks</b> in Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The emperors paid tribute in turn to the <b>Seljuks</b> of Rum, the Mongol Ilkhans and other regional powers, and they married their daughters to neighbouring Turkmen rulers, Georgian princes and Byzantine nobles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
